00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.539 align:start position:0%
the unexpected behavior of small  satellite galaxies is proving a headache  for current models of the structure of  the universe existing theories predict  that small galaxies orbiting around a  larger host galaxy should move pretty  much randomly but new research shows  that the galaxies orbiting Centaurus a  are on 12 million light-years from Earth  or circling in the same direction  researchers have observed that from our  earthbound perspective the satellite  galaxies on one side of Centaurus a tend  to be coming towards us while those on  the other side are moving away  indicating that they are rotating in the  same direction of the 16 satellite  galaxies examined 14 appeared to be  moving the same way but simulations of  galaxy formation only produced this  arrangement less than half of a percent  of the time astronomers have observed  similar galactic behavior closer to home  the galaxies orbiting both the Milky Way  and our nearest neighbor Andromeda also  seemed to rotate in the same direction  and in fin planes researchers say that  the likelihood that these three coherent  planes of galaxies all arose by chance  is astronomically small suggesting that  cosmological models may need tweaking  [Music]
 

